1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,865
The British Army already had a fast gun. And it went looking for a slower one. On purpose. ...

2
00:00:04,911 --> 00:00:12,573
That strange decision ended up changing what every army in the world expects from a soldier. ... This is the gun. The Baker rifle.

3
00:00:12,631 --> 00:00:16,347
With it, one man killed a French general in the snow... with a single shot.

4
00:00:16,393 --> 00:00:21,594
And fifteen years later, at Waterloo, it left hundreds of men in a farmhouse, holding off an army...

5
00:00:21,629 --> 00:00:25,774
with empty pouches. ... So which was it? A stroke of genius?

6
00:00:25,843 --> 00:00:28,514
Or a very expensive mistake? ... Stay with me.

7
00:00:28,595 --> 00:00:31,823
Because the answer is hiding in a detail you've seen a hundred times...

8
00:00:32,426 --> 00:00:36,316
and never noticed. ... January, 1809.

9
00:00:36,432 --> 00:00:42,666
Northern Spain. A British army is retreating through the snow. French cavalry right behind it, led by a young general.

10
00:00:42,710 --> 00:00:50,013
Auguste de Colbert. A soldier in a dark green jacket runs forward. Drops. Aims. ... Colbert falls from his horse.

11
00:00:50,291 --> 00:00:56,224
An officer rushes to help him. The rifleman has already reloaded. ... Second shot. Second man down. ...

12
00:00:56,665 --> 00:01:02,575
Keep that soldier in mind. We're coming back to him. And the truth about that shot isn't what the legend says. ...

13
00:01:02,691 --> 00:01:05,048
Picture a Napoleonic battle. Go on.

14
00:01:05,129 --> 00:01:10,621
You already have the image. Long red lines. Men shoulder to shoulder. Smoke everywhere. Right?

15
00:01:10,737 --> 00:01:13,247
Now look again. Look in front of the red line.

16
00:01:13,526 --> 00:01:18,878
There's something there most people never notice. Men in green. Spread out. Lying behind walls.

17
00:01:18,994 --> 00:01:24,242
Hiding in ditches. Not firing at a crowd. Choosing. ... Those men are the real story today.

18
00:01:24,312 --> 00:01:29,571
Because "practical" never meant "the best rifle ever made." Practical means something an army can actually live with.

19
00:01:29,896 --> 00:01:33,472
And that turned out to be much harder than building a good gun. ...

20
00:01:33,530 --> 00:01:41,428
Let me ask you something. If rifles were more accurate than muskets... why didn't every army use them? It's a fair question. Rifles weren't new.

21
00:01:41,451 --> 00:01:45,898
Hunters had been using them for centuries. The answer is simple. And a little embarrassing.

22
00:01:46,049 --> 00:01:52,898
... Rifles were a pain. First, they were slow. The ball had to fit tight, so it could grip the spiral grooves inside the barrel.

23
00:01:52,980 --> 00:01:59,946
You wrapped it in a greasy patch... and you forced it down. A musket ball? You just dropped it in. ... Second, they got dirty.

24
00:02:00,074 --> 00:02:02,466
Fast. Black powder leaves gunk behind.

25
00:02:02,515 --> 00:02:08,134
A few shots later, that tight ball won't go down anymore. ... And third, they were expensive. Handmade.

26
00:02:08,285 --> 00:02:15,448
Delicate. An army doesn't want delicate. An army wants ten thousand cheap guns that survive being dropped in the mud by a nineteen-year-old.

27
00:02:15,959 --> 00:02:20,696
... So generals made a choice. Forget aiming. Put a thousand men in a line.

28
00:02:20,812 --> 00:02:25,316
Fire as fast as you can. Something will hit. ... And look, be fair to them.

29
00:02:25,409 --> 00:02:29,148
If you were a general in 1790, you'd probably have made the same call.

30
00:02:29,299 --> 00:02:33,850
Speed wins. Numbers win. Everybody knows that. ... Right up until they don't.

31
00:02:33,873 --> 00:02:38,959
... And if you saw our Ferguson rifle episode, you know Britain had already had a chance to change this.

32
00:02:39,075 --> 00:02:45,181
A brilliant rifle. Fast. Accurate. And the army let it disappear. ... Then France changed the rules. ...

33
00:02:45,251 --> 00:02:51,061
In the 1790s, Revolutionary France had huge armies full of new, barely trained soldiers.

34
00:02:51,178 --> 00:02:53,755
They couldn't march in perfect lines like professionals.

35
00:02:53,801 --> 00:02:56,112
So the French did something clever. They let them loose.

36
00:02:56,356 --> 00:03:02,393
Swarms of skirmishers ran ahead of the main army. They hid behind hedges. Fired. Moved. Fired again. ...

37
00:03:02,474 --> 00:03:07,176
Now imagine you're a British officer standing in a perfect red line. You can't see the enemy.

38
00:03:07,211 --> 00:03:09,835
But the enemy can see you. And he's aiming...

39
00:03:10,276 --> 00:03:15,349
at you. ... That's not a fair fight. And Britain learned it the hard way, in Flanders.

40
00:03:15,431 --> 00:03:22,536
So the proud British Army did something it hated. It borrowed. It hired foreign riflemen, mostly Germans. ... But think about that for a second.

41
00:03:22,710 --> 00:03:26,332
The most powerful empire in the world... renting its sharpshooters.

42
00:03:26,483 --> 00:03:32,126
Imagine the pride that had to be swallowed for that. That couldn't last. Britain needed its own rifle. ...

43
00:03:32,161 --> 00:03:37,783
February, 1800. Woolwich. Near London. The army lines up rifles for a test.

44
00:03:38,166 --> 00:03:42,438
And one of them was made by a man you've probably never heard of. Ezekiel Baker.

45
00:03:42,612 --> 00:03:48,034
... Baker wasn't a general. He wasn't a famous inventor. He was a gunmaker from Whitechapel.

46
00:03:48,150 --> 00:03:51,204
A working man. The kind of guy who fixed your gun when it broke.

47
00:03:51,378 --> 00:03:54,060
... The number people usually quote from that trial?

48
00:03:54,269 --> 00:04:00,411
Eleven hits out of twelve. At three hundred yards. For 1800... that's excellent. But here's the twist.

49
00:04:00,562 --> 00:04:03,813
That's not why he won. ... Plenty of rifles could shoot straight.

50
00:04:03,963 --> 00:04:09,432
Germany was full of them. The army needed something else. A rifle that still worked after a march in the rain.

51
00:04:09,606 --> 00:04:15,550
After a soldier dropped it on a rock. After fifty dirty shots. ... And Baker gave them exactly that.

52
00:04:15,573 --> 00:04:20,995
By making his rifle... a little bit worse. ... Look at the grooves inside the barrel. Seven of them.

53
00:04:21,111 --> 00:04:24,496
Most people would say: more spin, more accuracy. Right?

54
00:04:24,577 --> 00:04:30,161
Baker went the other way. His grooves turned only a quarter of a circle down the whole barrel. Slow spin. ...

55
00:04:30,231 --> 00:04:36,570
That cost him a little accuracy at very long range. But the ball went down easier. Even when the barrel got dirty.

56
00:04:36,651 --> 00:04:42,084
Which means the soldier could keep fighting. ... Remember that tight fit. And that special way of loading.

57
00:04:42,328 --> 00:04:44,465
Today it's the rifle's greatest strength.

58
00:04:44,557 --> 00:04:50,769
Later... it becomes its biggest problem. ... Everything else followed the same idea. Shorter than a musket. Easier to handle.

59
00:04:50,792 --> 00:04:57,015
A little brass box in the stock for patches and tools. And because the gun was short, it got a long sword bayonet.

60
00:04:57,038 --> 00:05:00,498
The blade made up for what the barrel lacked. ... So here's the secret.

61
00:05:00,533 --> 00:05:05,211
Ezekiel Baker didn't build the best rifle in the world. He built a rifle the army couldn't break. ...

62
00:05:05,246 --> 00:05:09,786
And if this is already changing the picture in your head, do me a favor and subscribe.

63
00:05:09,867 --> 00:05:12,642
That's what we do here. We take the famous picture apart.

64
00:05:12,665 --> 00:05:20,104
... Because the gun... was only half the answer. Give that rifle to a normal redcoat, a man trained to fire fast and never think...

65
00:05:20,139 --> 00:05:25,132
And what do you get? A slow musket. ... So the army needed a new kind of soldier.

66
00:05:25,248 --> 00:05:31,284
... Two officers got the job. Coote Manningham and William Stewart. They pulled men from regiments all over the army.

67
00:05:31,400 --> 00:05:33,467
And they gave the unit a very honest name.

68
00:05:33,560 --> 00:05:37,705
The Experimental Corps of Riflemen. ... Experimental. Think about that.

69
00:05:37,737 --> 00:05:45,202
The army was admitting, out loud... "We don't know if this will work." ... It worked. A couple of years later, the experiment got a permanent number.

70
00:05:45,295 --> 00:05:50,682
The Ninety-Fifth. The Rifles. ... And what they built was the opposite of everything a redcoat was.

71
00:05:51,065 --> 00:05:57,729
The redcoat was told: don't think. Load. Aim at the mass. Fire on command. The rifleman was told: think.

72
00:05:57,880 --> 00:06:03,053
Judge the distance. Use the wall. Use the tree. Use the ditch. Pick your man. ...

73
00:06:03,122 --> 00:06:05,781
Now stop and think about how strange that was.

74
00:06:06,106 --> 00:06:10,471
For over a hundred years, the whole system depended on soldiers who didn't think for themselves.

75
00:06:10,506 --> 00:06:16,776
And suddenly the army is saying: we need you to think. We need you to decide. ... They fought in pairs.

76
00:06:16,950 --> 00:06:21,071
One fires. The other stays loaded. So the pair is never empty.

77
00:06:21,095 --> 00:06:26,006
They took orders by bugle, because in a scattered line you can't hear a drum. And they wore green.

78
00:06:26,122 --> 00:06:29,518
Dark green. Black buttons, not shiny brass. Why?

79
00:06:29,587 --> 00:06:35,578
Because they weren't there to look impressive. They were there to disappear. ... At a camp in Kent, Sir John Moore trained them.

80
00:06:35,694 --> 00:06:42,904
Officers had to know their men. Good shooting was rewarded. Soldiers were expected to use their heads. In that army? That was almost a revolution. ...

81
00:06:42,939 --> 00:06:49,243
And now you can see the pattern. Ferguson asked the army to accept his rifle. Manningham and Stewart did something smarter.

82
00:06:49,359 --> 00:06:55,794
They changed the army... so the rifle could fit. ... And that changed what a single shot was for. ... Here's a question for you.

83
00:06:55,817 --> 00:07:02,133
When a line of muskets fires... what is it aiming at? ... Nothing. Not really. It's aiming at a block of men.

84
00:07:02,412 --> 00:07:07,149
A thousand balls thrown at a wall, hoping enough of them land. ... A rifle is different.

85
00:07:07,300 --> 00:07:12,785
A rifle aims at a person. ... And on a battlefield, some people matter more than others.

86
00:07:13,168 --> 00:07:17,975
The officer giving the orders. The sergeant holding the line together. The gunner working the cannon.

87
00:07:18,044 --> 00:07:22,154
Take out one of them... and you haven't just removed a man. You've removed the decision.

88
00:07:22,177 --> 00:07:26,136
... That's the moment it clicks. The Baker was never trying to beat the musket at speed.

89
00:07:26,252 --> 00:07:33,207
It was playing a completely different game. ... Which brings us back to the snow. ... The rifleman at Cacabelos had a name. Thomas Plunket.

90
00:07:33,251 --> 00:07:39,532
An Irishman in the Ninety-Fifth. And his shot became a legend. You'll hear it everywhere.

91
00:07:39,811 --> 00:07:42,167
Six hundred yards. Sometimes more.

92
00:07:42,284 --> 00:07:49,098
... I'll be straight with you. That number grew with every retelling. Most historians say at least a hundred yards.

93
00:07:49,539 --> 00:07:53,289
Probably two or three hundred. Still a great shot. Just not a miracle.

94
00:07:53,336 --> 00:08:01,022
... But here's what the legend misses. The distance isn't the point. The point is that it was the plan, working perfectly.

95
00:08:01,080 --> 00:08:05,092
A trained man. Hidden. Choosing the one target that mattered most.

96
00:08:05,266 --> 00:08:07,831
One shot... and the attack lost its head.

97
00:08:07,924 --> 00:08:12,580
... So, what's your guess? How far was it really? Tell me in the comments. I read them. ...

98
00:08:12,661 --> 00:08:17,270
Now, here's the dangerous part of any success story. When a system works...

99
00:08:17,320 --> 00:08:22,591
people stop asking what it costs. ... In Spain and Portugal, the green jackets became famous.

100
00:08:23,102 --> 00:08:30,834
They were put together with the best light infantry into the Light Division, one of the most admired units in Wellington's army. Portugal copied the idea.

101
00:08:30,951 --> 00:08:38,184
Its best shots got Baker rifles too. The French skirmishers who used to bully British lines? Now they met men who shot back.

102
00:08:38,242 --> 00:08:40,958
And shot better. ... But remember that tight fit?

103
00:08:40,987 --> 00:08:43,622
That special loading? Here it comes. ...

104
00:08:43,692 --> 00:08:47,245
Every Baker needed the right size ball. The right patches.

105
00:08:47,361 --> 00:08:54,884
Good powder. Regular cleaning. And its ammunition wasn't the same as everyone else's. ... The army's supply carts were full of musket cartridges.

106
00:08:54,942 --> 00:08:58,402
Millions of them. Rifle ammunition? A separate item. Less of it.

107
00:08:58,437 --> 00:09:05,484
Fewer carts. ... In a small fight, nobody noticed. But what if a handful of riflemen had to hold one position...

108
00:09:05,658 --> 00:09:12,346
against an entire army... for hours... with nowhere to run? ... One summer afternoon in Belgium, that question got answered. ...

109
00:09:12,416 --> 00:09:18,859
June the eighteenth, 1815. Waterloo. ... In front of Wellington's army sits a small walled farm. La Haye Sainte.

110
00:09:18,894 --> 00:09:26,873
If the French take it, they're right at the heart of his line. ... Holding it is Major George Baring and about four hundred German soldiers fighting for Britain.

111
00:09:27,117 --> 00:09:32,910
Many of them carry the Baker rifle. ... The French come. Columns of them. Crashing around the walls like waves.

112
00:09:33,037 --> 00:09:37,426
The riflemen fire through tiny holes in the stone. The enemy is only a few yards away.

113
00:09:37,507 --> 00:09:43,486
The French fall back. ... Then they come again. ... Imagine being inside. Mud. Smoke.

114
00:09:43,524 --> 00:09:47,704
Screaming. A hole in the wall the size of your hand.

115
00:09:48,029 --> 00:09:53,160
And on the other side, so many men you barely need to aim. ... They aim anyway.

116
00:09:53,288 --> 00:09:57,584
At the officer pushing the column forward. At the drummer beating the advance.

117
00:09:57,967 --> 00:10:00,057
Because that's what they were trained to do. ...

118
00:10:00,103 --> 00:10:05,653
At one point the barn catches fire. They fight the flames with whatever can carry water. And they hold.

119
00:10:05,769 --> 00:10:10,633
Hour after hour... they hold. ... These aren't statues in a museum. These are young men.

120
00:10:10,842 --> 00:10:13,596
Tired. Burned. Deaf from the noise.

121
00:10:13,631 --> 00:10:18,972
And every one of them knows that if this farm falls, the man next to him probably dies. So they keep going. ...

122
00:10:19,100 --> 00:10:25,950
Each man started the day with about sixty rounds. Sixty sounds like a lot. ... It isn't. ... Baring sends for more ammunition.

123
00:10:26,333 --> 00:10:32,428
Nothing useful comes. He sends again. ... Nothing. ... The most common explanation takes us right back to that detail.

124
00:10:32,544 --> 00:10:37,818
Their rifle ammunition was different. And in the chaos behind the lines, it never arrived.

125
00:10:37,841 --> 00:10:44,900
Historians still argue about exactly what went wrong. But nobody argues about what happened next. ... The firing from the walls gets weaker.

126
00:10:45,051 --> 00:10:48,279
Men search the pouches of their dead friends for a single cartridge.

127
00:10:48,430 --> 00:10:51,715
... Think about that. You spent years learning to make every shot count.

128
00:10:51,750 --> 00:10:58,780
And now you're counting shots... you don't have. ... The French notice. ... Around six in the evening, they break in.

129
00:10:58,931 --> 00:11:04,039
... Baring gets the survivors out. Only a small part of the men who held that farm make it back.

130
00:11:04,109 --> 00:11:06,906
... ... So let me ask you. Was the Baker a failure? ...

131
00:11:06,941 --> 00:11:11,156
A lot of people would say yes. The rifle ran dry. The farm fell.

132
00:11:11,191 --> 00:11:15,371
Case closed. ... But look closer. The riflemen didn't break.

133
00:11:15,463 --> 00:11:20,096
The training didn't break. They fought until there was literally nothing left to fire.

134
00:11:20,154 --> 00:11:26,214
... What broke was the supply. An army built around muskets simply couldn't feed a few special rifles. ...

135
00:11:26,249 --> 00:11:32,031
And Wellington still won that day. But La Haye Sainte had exposed the rifle's last weakness.

136
00:11:32,123 --> 00:11:37,383
It was still special. ... And special things are hard to keep alive in war. ... So what happened next? ...

137
00:11:37,406 --> 00:11:40,544
In the late 1830s, the Baker was finally replaced.

138
00:11:40,718 --> 00:11:47,173
And then came the real game changer. A Frenchman named Claude-Étienne Minié helped develop a new kind of bullet.

139
00:11:47,347 --> 00:11:51,027
Hollow at the base. It slid down the barrel as easily as a musket ball.

140
00:11:51,271 --> 00:11:54,069
And when it fired, it expanded and gripped the grooves.

141
00:11:54,244 --> 00:12:00,281
... You see what that means? Fast like a musket. Accurate like a rifle. The old trade-off Baker had to live with...

142
00:12:00,327 --> 00:12:05,168
gone. ... By the 1850s, Britain was handing rifles to its ordinary soldiers.

143
00:12:05,215 --> 00:12:10,904
And it opened a shooting school in Kent. Just down the coast from where the Ninety-Fifth had trained fifty years earlier. ...

144
00:12:10,950 --> 00:12:17,010
Now every soldier had to do what the green jackets did. Aim. Judge distance. Hit a man, not a crowd. ... ...

145
00:12:17,092 --> 00:12:23,303
La Haye Sainte fell because the rifle was still special. Forty years later, rifles won... because they weren't special anymore. ...

146
00:12:23,338 --> 00:12:25,750
Step back for a second, and look at the whole thing.

147
00:12:25,796 --> 00:12:30,719
For three hundred years, rifles failed because armies expected them to act like muskets.

148
00:12:30,754 --> 00:12:33,296
Ferguson said: "Here's a brilliant gun. Accept it."

149
00:12:33,320 --> 00:12:38,359
The army said no. ... Baker went the other way. First, the gun bent to fit the army. Slower spin.

150
00:12:38,405 --> 00:12:45,263
Tougher parts. A design twenty workshops could copy. Then the army bent to fit the gun. New soldiers. New training. New trust.

151
00:12:45,286 --> 00:12:51,730
... That's what practical really means. Not the best machine. The machine people can live with. And people willing to change... to live with it.

152
00:12:51,753 --> 00:12:57,732
... And that's not just a military lesson. Look at almost anything that changed the world. The best idea rarely wins first.

153
00:12:57,790 --> 00:13:04,460
The one that people can actually live with... does. ... So next time you see a painting of those famous red lines...

154
00:13:04,843 --> 00:13:07,398
Don't look at the red. Look in front of it.

155
00:13:07,456 --> 00:13:09,963
That thin green line lying in the grass?

156
00:13:10,289 --> 00:13:16,012
That's the future. ... Let's go back to the snow one last time. ... A rifleman lies in the frozen mud.

157
00:13:16,140 --> 00:13:19,135
Reloading. A greasy patch. A lead ball.

158
00:13:19,164 --> 00:13:26,269
Seven grooves, turning a quarter of a circle. And one thing no musket soldier had ever been given. Permission to choose his own target. ...

159
00:13:26,304 --> 00:13:30,019
That's why his shot mattered. Not the distance. The idea. ...

160
00:13:30,623 --> 00:13:33,096
And here's the part the legend usually leaves out.

161
00:13:33,212 --> 00:13:39,284
Thomas Plunket, the man behind one of the most famous shots of the Napoleonic Wars, didn't end up rich or honored.

162
00:13:39,365 --> 00:13:42,477
He ended up with a tiny pension. And he died poor.

163
00:13:42,500 --> 00:13:45,356
... The idea lived forever. The man who proved it...

164
00:13:45,391 --> 00:13:50,372
was almost forgotten. ... So here's my question, and I want a real answer. What mattered more?

165
00:13:50,546 --> 00:13:55,991
The rifle Ezekiel Baker built... or the soldiers who were trained to use it? ... Tell me below.

166
00:13:56,165 --> 00:14:00,159
I'm curious where you land. ... And if you want the other side of this story...

167
00:14:00,310 --> 00:14:06,986
the brilliant rifle that came twenty years too early, and the army that walked away from it... The Ferguson rifle episode is right here.
